so passes through (3,3) not needed. So L1 covers (1,1) and (2,2). Good.\n\nL2: line through (1,2) and (3,1) slope (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2, which is not 0, -1, infinite, so sunny. It also passes through (2,1.5) not integer. So L2 covers (1,2) and (3,1). Good.\n\nL3: line through (1,3) and (2,1) slope (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, sunny. It also passes through (3,-1) not needed. So L3 covers (1,3) and (2,1). So all points are covered: (1,1) L1, (1,2) L2, (1,3) L3, (2,1) L3, (2,2) L1, (2,3) ??? Wait (2,3) is not a required point because a+b ≤ 4 => (2,3) sum =5 >4, so not required. So we have covered all required points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1). Indeed L1 covers (1,1),(2,2); L2 covers (1,2),(3,1); L3 covers (1,3),(2,1). So all points covered. So k=3 works for n=3.\n\nNow for n=4, k=3: we need 4 lines total, 3 sunny, 1 non-sunny. The solution says construction exists using three sunny lines. Let's try to construct for n=4.\n\nPoints: column 1: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4); column 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3); column 3: (3,1),(3,2); column 4: (4,1). Need 4 lines, exactly 3 sunny, 1 non-sunny. The non-sunny line can be vertical covering a column fully. Likely we use vertical line x=1 (non-sunny) covering column 1 (4 points). Then we need 3 sunny lines to cover remaining points: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1). That's 6 points. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, so each line can cover at most 3 points (since there are columns 2,3,4). With 3 lines, total capacity = 9 > 6, so possible. Need to assign points to lines such that each line is straight.\n\nWe can try to assign:\n\n- L1 (sunny) covers (2,1) and (3,2) maybe? Let's see slope: (2,1) to (3,2) slope = (2-1)/(3-2) = 1, sunny. Then at x=4, y = 1 + (4-2)*1 = 3, not needed. So L1 covers (2,1),(3,2).\n\n- L2 (sunny) covers (2,2) and (4,1). Slope = (1-2)/(4-2) = -1/2, sunny. At x=3, y = 2 + (3-2)*(-0.5) = 1.5, not needed. So L2 covers (2,2),(4,1).\n\n- L3 (sunny) covers (2,3) and (3,1). Slope = (1-3)/(3-2) = -2, sunny.