iagonal has points (a,b) with a≥1, b≥1, a+b=c, so there are c-1 points (since a can be 1,...,c-1). So diagonal c has c-1 points.\n\nThus the shape is symmetric.\n\nWe need to cover all points with n lines. Let's think about the minimal number of lines needed to cover all points. Since we have n lines, we need to be efficient.\n\nOne trivial solution is to use n vertical lines x=1,...,x=n. That uses 0 sunny lines. So k=0 works.\n\nAnother trivial solution is to use n horizontal lines y=1,...,y=n. That also uses 0 sunny lines (horizontal lines are non-sunny). So k=0 works.\n\nWe can also use a mix of vertical and horizontal lines. For example, we could use vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-1 and a horizontal line y=1 to cover column n's point (n,1). That uses n lines (n-1 vertical + 1 horizontal). That's also k=0.\n\nThus k=0 is definitely possible.\n\nNow, can we have k=1? Yes, we can replace one of the vertical lines with a sunny line that covers some points not covered by the remaining vertical lines. For example, we could use vertical lines x=1,...,x=n-1 (n-1 lines) and a sunny line that passes through (n,1) and some other point not already covered. That works. So k=1 is possible.\n\nNow, can we have k=2? We need to have exactly 2 sunny lines and n-2 non-sunny lines. We need to find a configuration.\n\nWe need to consider the constraints: each sunny line can intersect each column at most once (since it's not vertical). So each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. So with 2 sunny lines, we can cover at most 2 points per column. For columns with more than 2 points, we need a non-sunny line (vertical) to cover the rest. So columns with >2 points must have a vertical line. Which columns have >2 points? Columns a with n+1-a > 2 => a < n-1. So columns a = 1,...,n-2 have >2 points (since n+1-a > 2 => a < n-1). Let's check: For a = n-2, n+1-(n-2) = 3 points. So column n-2 has exactly 3 points. So columns a = 1,...,n-2 have at least 3 points. So they need ve